@startuml proto-nec-ir-timing
' ===========================================================================
' proto-nec-ir-timing.puml - (c) NEC wire timing, in the real numbers.
'
' Measured off the pin by tb/tb_pe_soc_ir_nec.v on real RTL (60 MHz):
'   carrier            38 049 Hz (nominal 38 000, +0.128 %)
'   LOW half           13.133-13.249 us  (787.95-794.95 clocks)
'   HIGH half          13.149 us          (788.95 clocks, spread 0.01)
'   half-period spread 7.00 clocks LOW, 0.01 HIGH, over 521 pulses
'   leader             8 988.0 us in 343 carrier cycles
'   leader gap         4 504.0 us
'   data bursts        eight at 552.0 us
'   gaps               four long (a zero) and four short (a one)
'   stop burst         552.0 us in 22 carrier cycles
'   decoded            a5
' ===========================================================================

title NEC infrared - wire timing\n<font size=12>carrier 38 049 Hz; halves 787.95-794.95 and 788.95 clocks, compared against EACH OTHER</font>

hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #33555
  LifeLineBorderColor #33555
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #FAFAFA
  ParticipantBorderColor #33555
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #F4F7FB
  ParticipantFontName monospaced
  ActorFontName monospaced
  NoteFontName monospaced
  GroupFontName monospaced
}

participant "CPU\npe_cpu.v" as CPU
participant "pad 6\nTXPIN / PINOE" as PAD
participant "IR LED\ncathode on the pin:\nLOW emits" as LED
participant "the air\n38 kHz" as AIR
participant "a receiver\nno clock, no wire" as RECV

== the carrier: the one thing that cannot be a little bit late ==

CPU -> PAD : PINOE = IR_DATA - the LED is driven
note over PAD
  <b>LOW emits.</b> The cathode is on the pad, so the
  levels are inverted relative to every other act in
  this family. Open-drain is deliberately NOT set:
  there is nothing to contend with on an LED.
end note
note over CPU
  THE CARRIER IS FITTED TO THE CLOCK, NOT TO MICROSECONDS.

  A half period at 38 kHz is
    60e6 / (2 * 38000) = 789.47 clocks
  so 789 clocks is <b>38.0228 kHz</b>, <b>+0.06 %</b>, and
  there is NO WAY TO BE WRONG BY MORE THAN HALF A
  CLOCK if the constant is one of 789 or 790.
end note
group one carrier cycle - two half periods, fitted SEPARATELY
  CPU -> PAD : ph9: TXPIN = 0x00 - source, the HIGH half
  note over CPU
    <b>IR_H1 = 5 on the (2,44) pair.</b>
    one outer step = 10 + (4*44 + 7) = <b>193 clocks</b>
    ladder 13 + 4*193 + 4 = 13 + 776 = <b>789 clocks</b>
  end note
  note over LED
    <b>HIGH half measured 788.95 clocks = 13.149 us</b>
    spread over 521 pulses: <b>0.01 clocks</b>
  end note
  CPU -> PAD : ph0: TXPIN = 0x00 - sink, the LOW half
  note over CPU
    <b>IR_H2 = 6 on the (2,34) pair.</b>
    one outer step = 10 + (4*34 + 7) = <b>153 clocks</b>
    ladder 20 + 5*153 + 4 = 20 + 769 = <b>789 clocks</b>
  end note
  note over LED
    <b>LOW half measured 787.95 - 794.95 clocks</b>
    spread: <b>7.00 clocks</b> - and that spread is
    the subject of the check below, not an error
  end note
end group
RECV -> AIR : the carrier
note over RECV
  A carrier 1 % off is not a carrier. A gap 3 % long is
  still a gap a receiver calls a zero - which is why
  the CARRIER is the quantity fitted to the clock and
  the GAPS are the quantities fitted to microseconds,
  and why they are not fitted the same way.
end note

== the leader: 9 ms, split in two because of the eight-bit immediate ==

CPU -> LED : 342 carrier cycles
note over CPU
  <b>LDI A, 342 ASSEMBLED TO LDI A, 86</b> - the ISA's
  immediate is EIGHT BITS, and 342 & 0xFF = 86.

  The frame that left the pin was a perfectly good
  38 kHz carrier whose 9 ms leader was <b>2.3 ms</b>.
  Nothing downstream noticed: the carrier was right,
  the bursts were right, the gaps were right. Only
  the LEADER was wrong, and only because a count
  did not fit in an immediate.
end note
note over LED
  <b>measured 8 988.0 us in 343 carrier cycles</b>

  Hence IR_LEADR = 2 runs of IR_LEAD = 171 cycles
  each: 2 x 171 = 342.
end note
CPU -> PAD : PINOE = 0x00 - RELEASE
note over LED
  <b>4.5 ms of silence: measured 4 504.0 us</b>

  This gap is what tells a receiver a frame is
  STARTING rather than continuing.
end note
note over CPU
  <b>IR_LEADGAP = 255 ON THE (3,130) PAIR, NOT (4,40).</b>
  (4,40) wants 529 outer steps for 4.5 ms, and
  529 & 0xFF is 17 - so the leader's gap came out
  <b>136 us</b> instead of 4.5 ms.

  And 136 us does not look like a truncated constant.
  It looks like a TRANSMITTER THAT IS IN A HURRY,
  which is the worst kind of wrong: plausible, small,
  and in the one place a receiver uses for frame
  detection. (3,130) has a 1 064-clock step, so 255
  outer steps reach the target inside one byte:
  254 * 1064 + 4 = <b>270 260 clocks = 4 504.33 us</b>.
end note

== the eight data bits: the value IS the silence ==

group a 0-bit - short burst, LONG silence
  CPU -> LED : IR_BIT = 21 carrier cycles
  note over LED
    <b>552.0 us of 38 kHz</b>
  end note
  CPU -> PAD : release
  note over LED
    <b>1 694.9 us of silence</b> (datasheet 1 687.5)
    IR_GAP0 = 200 on (4,40): 199*511 + 4 = 101 693 clk
  end note
  note over RECV
    the receiver times the SILENCE, and a long one is
    a zero. 199*511 + 4 = 101 693 clocks = 1 694.88 us.
  end note
end group

group a 1-bit - short burst, SHORT silence
  CPU -> LED : the same 552.0 us burst
  CPU -> PAD : release
  note over LED
    <b>562.2 us of silence</b> (datasheet 562.5)
    IR_GAP1 = 67 on (4,40): 66*511 + 4 = 33 730 clk
  end note
  note over RECV
    a short silence is a one. 66*511 + 4 = 33 730
    clocks = 562.17 us.
  end note
end group

note over CPU, RECV
  0xA5 = 1010 0101, <b>LSB first</b>:
  1,0,1,0,0,1,0,1 - four ones and four zeros, which is
  exactly what the TB found: <b>gaps 4 long (a zero) and
  4 short (a one)</b>, and <b>6 bit-transitions in 7</b>.
  ...
  The burst is IDENTICAL for both values, so the value is
  carried entirely by the silence. The bit is the rotating
  copy's own bit 0 and the copy is rotated right after each
  slot, so there is no shifting to get wrong.
end note

== the stop burst: the part a "send the byte" firmware leaves out ==

CPU -> LED : one final burst
note over LED
  <b>552.0 us in 22 carrier cycles</b>, then release.
end note
note over RECV
  <b>WITHOUT IT A RECEIVER CANNOT TELL THE FRAME ENDED
  rather than that the remote is transmitting a very
  long gap.</b> It is the part of a frame a firmware
  written for "send the byte" usually leaves out, and
  the TB counts the carrier cycles to see it is there.
end note

== the check the mutation gate forced into the TB ==

note over CPU, RECV
  <b>"Each half is constant" IS NOT "the two halves are EQUAL".</b>
  ...
  The gate's first run had three survivors of eleven. One put BOTH half periods
  on the same delay pair, which produced a carrier of 788 clocks one way and 782
  the other: <b>38.05 kHz against 37.88, 0.4 % asymmetric on every edge</b> -
  inside every frequency window a real receiver has - and it PASSED.
  ...
  A receiver does not care that the carrier is on frequency. It cares that the
  carrier is a CARRIER, and one whose halves differ is a square wave at 38 kHz
  with the wrong duty cycle. So the TB now measures the two DISTRIBUTIONS
  <b>against each other</b>, to within a clock, and that check is the act's
  whole point.
  ...
  The other two survivors were a burst of 20 carrier cycles (526 us, -6.4 %,
  which the old +/-10 % window accepted - now +/-5 %, which admits the two widths
  a whole number of cycles can produce, 21 at -1.7 % and 22 at +2.9 %, and
  rejects the third) and a mutant that was BEHAVIOURALLY A NO-OP because its
  anchor was written against the byte's LOAD rather than its ROTATE.
end note

note over CPU, RECV
  <b>A "whole number of carrier cycles" check was written and then DELETED as
  unsound.</b> The burst opens with a two-instruction initiation pulse, so its
  span is not a whole number of periods - and the check fired on all ten bursts
  INCLUDING THE CORRECT ONES.

  A check that is wrong about what it measures is worse than no check: the first
  thing anyone would do is loosen the tolerance until it went away, and then the
  loosened tolerance is the one that is believed.
end note

@enduml
